00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:21.419
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:04:21.419 --> 00:04:22.140
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:22.530
Awesome.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.071
MELANIE WARRICK:
Well, this is great.

00:04:24.071 --> 00:04:24.780
So we're at Next.

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.530
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.170
MELANIE WARRICK: And you've
been here for two days.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.240
It's like the third
day, as we speak.

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.277
How has it been?

00:04:31.277 --> 00:04:33.360
What have you seen that
you've been excited about?

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:34.330
VIVIAN SAN: I think
this is literally

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:36.215
the biggest conference
we've ever been to,

00:04:36.215 --> 00:04:38.590
because we're only so used to
just being in one building.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.410
But this is spread out between
like five different places.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.870
And it's pretty
crazy, but I mean,

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.130
it's just insane how much
technologies are out there,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:49.049
and how much they're
releasing so quickly.

00:04:49.049 --> 00:04:51.090
I mean, it's also really
overwhelming [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.050
but really exciting,
because you know,

00:04:53.050 --> 00:04:54.900
especially now as
we're developers,

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.240
we know all these tools
that are out there.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.230
And we know that we can
take what we learn from here

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:03.460
and be able to tell
our companies about it,

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.160
be able to leverage
our knowledge

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.517
and being able to contribute.

00:05:06.517 --> 00:05:08.850
And I think that's really the
most amazing thing is just

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.017
being able to feel that way.

00:05:10.017 --> 00:05:11.641
STEPHANIE CUETO:
Yeah, and I think also

00:05:11.641 --> 00:05:12.930
just how welcoming it is too.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.182
I think sometimes we take
for granted that we're

00:05:15.182 --> 00:05:17.140
in San Francisco, and
then we meet other people

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.641
from other parts of the world
and they're like, oh, my gosh.

00:05:19.641 --> 00:05:20.640
They're taking pictures.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.020
And at least for me, I've
been to other conferences too.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:24.430
And this is probably
one of the biggest ones

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:25.450
where it combines
a lot of people,

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:27.280
because it's like
developers, marketing, sales,

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.620
like all these people that
come from different backgrounds

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:30.180
in tech.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.240
So it's pretty interesting
to just see the mesh of it.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.150
But when we talk to them,
they're like, oh, my god.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:35.170
You're from-- like
you live here.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:35.961
And I'm like, yeah.

00:05:35.961 --> 00:05:36.730
This is amazing.

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:37.980
You're like around everything.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:39.682
And I'm like, yeah,
it's really cool.

00:05:39.682 --> 00:05:41.890
So it's interesting just to
meet other diverse people

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.970
and just kind of get
the other perspective,

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:45.970
because sometimes I think
we do take for granted

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:47.090
that we're around here.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:47.770
MELANIE WARRICK:
And I was asking you

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:49.464
before we started
that, were there

00:05:49.464 --> 00:05:51.880
any sessions that you went to
that you were excited about?

00:05:51.880 --> 00:05:53.463
Are there any sessions
that you wanted

00:05:53.463 --> 00:05:55.840
to mention that you saw
and found very interesting?

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.464
STEPHANIE CUETO:
Yeah, so I was talking

00:05:57.464 --> 00:05:59.410
about the #IamRemarkable
workshop that's

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:01.960
happening upstairs in the
second forum, Moscone West.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:03.430
And it was just very--

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.770
I came in that it just kind
of like, oh, it sounds cool.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.300
Let me see what it's all about.

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.424
And then they came at you
with stats, they're like,

00:06:09.424 --> 00:06:11.750
hey, your remark was-- so
it's a workshop targeted

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.500
towards women, but
it's also for everyone.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.670
It's just kind of like
change your mindset

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:18.070
of how you think about
yourself, because with women

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:21.280
specifically, they are
more prone to be put down

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.240
by both men and women.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.270
So if they ever are
bragging about themselves

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:27.529
when they say their
accomplishments out loud,

00:06:27.529 --> 00:06:29.320
or if they speak up,
or anything like that,

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.540
they're more likely to be,
she's talking too much,

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:33.770
or she's like bragging
about herself, or anything.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:37.670
So you're getting hit not
only from the male side of it,

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:41.170
but also your own
colleagues that are women.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:42.889
And then on top of
that, we also are

00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:44.680
more prone to talk bad
about ourselves too.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:46.196
We don't want to
come off that way,

00:06:46.196 --> 00:06:48.070
so then we start putting
our own selves down.

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:50.070
Like, oh, we shouldn't
probably shine that much,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.390
and just put our accomplishments
a little bit down.

00:06:52.390 --> 00:06:55.327
So they gave us a
good background on it

00:06:55.327 --> 00:06:57.160
and just where these
stats were coming from.

00:06:57.160 --> 00:06:59.200
And then after that, we
did the actual workshop

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:00.908
where they gave us a
piece of paper where

00:07:00.908 --> 00:07:02.680
you write down all
the things that you're

00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:04.360
remarkable because of.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.780
So it was interesting just to
see like how people get stumped

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.640
and how people were
also very positive on.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.610
So they were able to
write the whole page down

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:15.397
to say I'm remarkable because
I'm a software engineer.

00:07:15.397 --> 00:07:17.980
I'm remarkable because I speak
two languages, three languages,

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.160
or four languages.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.580
I'm remarkable because I
volunteer all my spare time

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:22.129
here.

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:24.670
And just all these things that
come out that you don't really

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:26.294
realize that you're
doing, and that you

00:07:26.294 --> 00:07:27.550
are remarkable because.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.592
And so some people were
going really strong

00:07:29.592 --> 00:07:31.550
and some people were like
stumped at one point,

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:32.986
because we just--

00:07:32.986 --> 00:07:35.110
I guess we're prone to
not-- like they were saying,

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:37.180
we're not prone to
really think that way.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.590
And we need to change
that, look in the mirror,

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:42.970
be self-positive, talk
to yourself and say,

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.750
you are remarkable.

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.480
Yeah, you're beautiful,
you're positive.

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:49.480
No, you're an amazing person,
and change that perspective

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.610
a little bit, or more.

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:53.260
And so it was just amazing.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:55.510
I was almost able to write
that whole page at the end,

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.590
but kind of fell short.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:57.250
But it's OK.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:58.333
I'm going to try to do it.

00:07:58.333 --> 00:08:01.741
And it's a good thing to do when
you go to your teams and stuff

00:08:01.741 --> 00:08:02.240
like that.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:04.690
And even your
friends, make everyone

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.760
change their perspective
of how awesome they are.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:07.676
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:08:07.676 --> 00:08:09.620
Vivian, any sessions
that stood out for you?

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.050
VIVIAN SAN: Yeah, I mean that
one for sure was one of them

00:08:11.050 --> 00:08:11.410
too.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:12.868
We also went to
one where they were

00:08:12.868 --> 00:08:15.197
talking about accessibility
and inclusion.

00:08:15.197 --> 00:08:16.780
And I thought that
was really awesome,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.970
because just like you don't--

00:08:18.970 --> 00:08:21.280
especially when you're making
docs or making meetings,

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.200
that's not something
that you purposely

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.060
try to do and not make
it inclusive for all

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:27.435
your employees, but it's just
something that does happen.

00:08:27.435 --> 00:08:30.060
But it's like I found
it so amazing how

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.840
there was so much
detail that goes

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:34.780
into it to try to make
everyone feel safe

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.120
and make everyone feel included.

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:39.610
And I felt, especially for us,
it's like what we were told.

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:41.530
When we come into
this, we can learn

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.069
all the technical knowledge
that people will tell us

00:08:44.069 --> 00:08:45.610
and we can learn it
online and stuff,

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.760
but you can't really
teach people perspective.

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.010
And I think for us,
especially, coming in

00:08:51.010 --> 00:08:53.770
from non-technical background
and low income families

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.380
and communities, I
think that showed us

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.480
that we could contribute.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.570
And I felt like just
because of that talk,

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.870
it made me feel more comfortable
going and saying yes,

00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:06.640
I can make a
contribution, that I could

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.500
be a strength to this company.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:11.240
And I thought that was amazing
just to hear them say that.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:12.490
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:13.000
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.190
Have you played with any
of the showcase demos

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:16.810
or anything else
around here as well?

00:09:16.810 --> 00:09:17.200
VIVIAN SAN: Not yet.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:17.950
STEPHANIE CUETO: Not yet.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.195
But we're going to today.

00:09:19.195 --> 00:09:20.440
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:20.440 --> 00:09:22.274
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
what's next for you.

00:09:22.274 --> 00:09:23.189
STEPHANIE CUETO: Yeah.

00:09:23.189 --> 00:09:25.450
We've been going to a lot
of the talks and everything,

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:26.140
walking around.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:27.349
We haven't even gone to the--

00:09:27.349 --> 00:09:29.264
I think there's a couple
workshops that I want

00:09:29.264 --> 00:09:30.790
to go to today at the hotel.

00:09:30.790 --> 00:09:32.830
So I went to one in the
Metreon, in the theater,

00:09:32.830 --> 00:09:33.800
which was kind of interesting.

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:34.730
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:09:34.730 --> 00:09:37.240
STEPHANIE CUETO: And then
some of the hotels, there's--

00:09:37.240 --> 00:09:38.140
huge, you know.

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:39.130
MELANIE WARRICK: I know,
when we-- actually,

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.546
before we started, too, we
were talking about how you were

00:09:41.546 --> 00:09:44.290
surprised that there's so
much that Google has in terms

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:45.487
of products and services.

00:09:45.487 --> 00:09:47.820
STEPHANIE CUETO: Yeah, I
didn't know it was like an AWS,

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:48.730
basically, like Amazon.

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:49.750
I was like, whoa,
you can do everything

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.082
that Amazon does too, like
hosting websites, doing

00:09:52.082 --> 00:09:52.790
all these things.

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:53.420
I was like, whoa.

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:54.550
I didn't know all of this.

00:09:54.550 --> 00:09:57.220
And I was amazed when I was
hearing the keynote speakers

00:09:57.220 --> 00:10:00.070
